1
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:13,031
All right,
this time we're going to talk about,

2
00:00:13,031 --> 00:00:17,479
the lack of a better word or
title, Mapping IP Addresses.

3
00:00:17,479 --> 00:00:21,849
We're going to learn about some of
the more administrative aspects

4
00:00:21,849 --> 00:00:23,697
of the kind of IP addresses.

5
00:00:23,697 --> 00:00:29,002
That's how they're allocated,
how they relate to the MAC addresses.

6
00:00:29,002 --> 00:00:33,230
And how they get assigned
when you're on a network.

7
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:37,203
So where this fits in,

8
00:00:37,203 --> 00:00:42,922
this is kind of the overview of
when we introduced this module.

9
00:00:42,922 --> 00:00:43,594
And so we're here.

10
00:00:43,594 --> 00:00:49,446
How are our addresses assigned and
mapped to the link layer addresses?

11
00:00:49,446 --> 00:00:52,350
So first, talking about allocation.

12
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:56,874
So part of what kind of makes IP addresses

13
00:00:56,874 --> 00:01:01,798
useful is they have
this property that it's

14
00:01:01,798 --> 00:01:07,256
a effectively globally
unique address to be able

15
00:01:07,256 --> 00:01:13,550
to reach any device or
any point in the internet.

16
00:01:13,550 --> 00:01:17,600
And so there's kind of
an organization called IANA,

17
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:24,122
the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority
that performs this global coordination.

18
00:01:24,122 --> 00:01:29,541
And then you have local registries
called Regional Registries or

19
00:01:29,541 --> 00:01:33,978
RIRs that manage the local
IP address allocation.

20
00:01:33,978 --> 00:01:39,806
So an example of one of these
in North America would be ARIN.

21
00:01:39,806 --> 00:01:44,571
And so you can go to their website
shown here and make a request for

22
00:01:44,571 --> 00:01:47,450
an IP address allocation.

23
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:51,662
So in that process,
because of the scarcity,

24
00:01:51,662 --> 00:01:57,788
especially of IPV4 addresses,
you have to justify all of that.

25
00:01:57,788 --> 00:02:00,995
So that's if you want
to get them directly.

26
00:02:00,995 --> 00:02:05,067
But then also
the Internet Service Providers or

27
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:12,530
ISPs own large blocks of addresses and
they can provide them to their customers.

28
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:17,710
And so that's another way
to get some IP addresses.

29
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:28,070
Now the next kind of part of this is how
do we map an IP address to a MAC address.

30
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:31,869
So we talked about on
a Local Area Network.

31
00:02:31,869 --> 00:02:34,776
So we have a laptop X and
another one, Y and

32
00:02:34,776 --> 00:02:37,694
some Ethernet switches in between them.

33
00:02:37,694 --> 00:02:41,676
And we showed that when
X wants to talk to Y,

34
00:02:41,676 --> 00:02:48,990
it will put the destination MAC address
as whatever Y's MAC address is.

35
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:54,854
But the chances are X,
you probably know the IP address

36
00:02:54,854 --> 00:03:01,500
of Y of some service more likely
than knowing its MAC address.

37
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:06,516
And even more likely you
know the name of the server,

38
00:03:06,516 --> 00:03:10,621
which we'll know when we cover DNS later.

39
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:15,410
But what this provides is
consistent communication

40
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:19,978
in a larger network if
we're using IP addresses or

41
00:03:19,978 --> 00:03:23,692
server names instead of MAC addresses.

42
00:03:23,692 --> 00:03:29,159
But the actual communication at the link
layer is done using MAC addresses.

43
00:03:29,159 --> 00:03:34,760
So we need a way for X to be able to
find out what is Y's MAC address.

44
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:40,275
And so the solution to this
is a protocol called ARP for

45
00:03:40,275 --> 00:03:43,070
Address Resolution Protocol.

46
00:03:43,070 --> 00:03:51,476
And so how ARP works is when the laptop
on the left wants to send out a packet,

47
00:03:51,476 --> 00:03:55,360
send a message to some IP address.

48
00:03:56,530 --> 00:04:00,778
Inside of the operating system
is a table called an ARP table.

49
00:04:00,778 --> 00:04:03,341
It will look up in the ARP table and say,

50
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:07,160
do I know the MAC address for
this particular IP address?

51
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:12,205
And if so,
it will put that inside of the frame and

52
00:04:12,205 --> 00:04:15,830
send that into the network.

53
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:20,146
If it's not there,
then it has to use the ARP protocol.

54
00:04:20,146 --> 00:04:25,246
And so how this works is it
broadcasts basically a message

55
00:04:25,246 --> 00:04:29,719
that's saying I'm looking for
some IP address.

56
00:04:29,719 --> 00:04:33,584
And then asking the question
what is your MAC address?

57
00:04:33,584 --> 00:04:35,382
And so it sends that into the network.

58
00:04:35,382 --> 00:04:41,837
And if we recall about broadcast,
it will forward it out all of the ports.

59
00:04:41,837 --> 00:04:44,600
And each of the destinations or

60
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:49,600
each of the kind of nodes on
the network will receive it.

61
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:55,486
Only the one that actually
has that IP address that's

62
00:04:55,486 --> 00:04:58,790
being looked for will respond.

63
00:04:58,790 --> 00:05:07,279
And so in here it's the one on the laptop
on the right that's shown says that's me,

64
00:05:07,279 --> 00:05:12,753
my MAC address is and
then it fills in its MAC address.

65
00:05:12,753 --> 00:05:13,608
All right and

66
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:18,360
that's a real simple protocol, but
that's effectively how it works.

67
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:22,934
And we could see so
shown here is a Wireshark capture.

68
00:05:22,934 --> 00:05:27,397
So Wireshark is a tool that
you can use to capture

69
00:05:27,397 --> 00:05:32,432
traffic on a network from
a network interface card.

70
00:05:32,432 --> 00:05:35,146
And we can see a whole
bunch of ARP traffic.

71
00:05:35,146 --> 00:05:39,550
And then if you zoom in we could
see the Ethernet frame and

72
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:43,650
we could see the destination
is set to all f's.

73
00:05:43,650 --> 00:05:46,870
That means it's a broadcast.

74
00:05:46,870 --> 00:05:52,888
And it's this request of tell me.

75
00:05:52,888 --> 00:05:56,581
So I'm the sender,
here's my MAC address, but

76
00:05:56,581 --> 00:06:02,890
the IP address that I'm looking for
is 192.168.200.200.

77
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:06,818
And it's asking for the reply.

78
00:06:06,818 --> 00:06:08,896
And then somewhere else,

79
00:06:08,896 --> 00:06:14,570
another message would be that whoever
has that address would respond.

80
00:06:16,750 --> 00:06:21,334
Then the last piece is how do
IP addresses get assigned?

81
00:06:21,334 --> 00:06:27,172
So I have a laptop, I join a network,
I have an IP address.

82
00:06:27,172 --> 00:06:28,740
So how did it get there?

83
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:33,012
So there's kind of two main
ways that this would happen.

84
00:06:33,012 --> 00:06:36,312
One, would be you statically assign it.

85
00:06:36,312 --> 00:06:41,038
So you can go into your operating
system and shown here as a command.

86
00:06:41,038 --> 00:06:48,942
In Linux and assign a specific IP
address to a network interface.

87
00:06:48,942 --> 00:06:51,240
And so this is showing for eth0.

88
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:58,675
The network interface eth0, it's showing
to set the IP address of ten 10.1.1.10.

89
00:06:58,675 --> 00:07:03,334
And why it's showing it has
a prefix is it'll add a routing

90
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:08,004
table entry that kind of covers
anything in that network.

91
00:07:08,004 --> 00:07:11,576
It's basically saying I'm
part of that Subnet, so

92
00:07:11,576 --> 00:07:14,510
add an entry to the routing table locally.

93
00:07:15,890 --> 00:07:21,380
The other main way would be dynamically
and this is a lot more common.

94
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:25,150
And so there's a protocol called DHCP.

95
00:07:25,150 --> 00:07:29,110
For Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol.

96
00:07:29,110 --> 00:07:34,538
And this is something that as a client,
when you connect to a network,

97
00:07:34,538 --> 00:07:37,788
a lot of times it just
runs as part of that.

98
00:07:37,788 --> 00:07:44,482
But a lot of times there's a server on
your network called the DHCP server.

99
00:07:44,482 --> 00:07:51,941
And it's commonly the gateway router but
the protocol goes basically DHCP discover,

100
00:07:51,941 --> 00:07:56,140
which is you asking to be
assigned an IP address.

101
00:07:57,150 --> 00:08:00,771
The response would be an offer and

102
00:08:00,771 --> 00:08:05,354
it would say great, I'm a DHCP server.

103
00:08:05,354 --> 00:08:07,572
Here's something you could choose.

104
00:08:07,572 --> 00:08:10,164
Now why you have this offer is,

105
00:08:10,164 --> 00:08:15,062
well there might be multiple
DHCP servers on the network.

106
00:08:15,062 --> 00:08:20,759
And then you finally choose one of
the offers and then you make the request,

107
00:08:20,759 --> 00:08:25,658
which would get to the server and
then the client acknowledges.

108
00:08:25,658 --> 00:08:29,288
So this is all built into
the operating system,

109
00:08:29,288 --> 00:08:34,840
happens automatically, but
this is the protocol that is actually run.

110
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:37,404
So with that,

111
00:08:37,404 --> 00:08:43,610
we kind of saw some of the more
administrative aspects of IP addresses.

112
00:08:43,610 --> 00:08:46,124
And with that, we'll end here.

113
00:08:46,124 --> 00:08:47,450
All right, thanks.

114
00:08:47,450 --> 00:08:47,950
Bye.